{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Usability Testing and Learning Evidence", "title": "Learning Metrics and Usability Metrics", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting learning metrics and usability metrics to moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence", "ail606_supplement_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 4, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "05", "section": "04", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/05/03"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_5_4_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_5_4_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_5_4_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_5_4_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics_calibration"]}, "content": "# Learning Metrics and Usability Metrics\n\n![Learning Metrics and Usability Metrics textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/05-04-learning-metrics-and-usability-metrics.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA satisfied learner is not necessarily a learning learner, and an efficient interface is not necessarily an effective lesson. The single most common overclaim in instructional design is letting one comfortable number (usually satisfaction) speak for a claim it cannot support. This section is about construct validity in measurement: matching each claim you want to make to a metric that can actually bear it. Usability metrics tell you whether people can operate the thing; learning metrics tell you whether they walked away able to do something they could not do before. They answer different questions, and a plan that confuses them will report success on the easy question while staying silent on the hard one.\n\nIn AIL 606 the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace must keep these two measurement families distinct and pair each claim with the metric that fits it. Mixing them is not a rounding error; it is a category mistake that lets a smooth, well-liked module pass without any evidence that anyone learned.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Classify a metric as usability (operability) or learning (capability gain) by the construct it measures.\n- Pair each claim with a metric that has the validity to support it, never a proxy.\n- Read satisfaction as an attitude signal, not as evidence of learning or of effectiveness.\n- Combine completion, time, error, and outcome data into a picture no single number could give.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA team tests a statistics-tutorial prototype and reports that nine of ten learners rated it four or five out of five. The headline writes itself: the module works and learners are learning. The slide deck is built before anyone notices the gap.\n\nThe gap is that satisfaction measured how the experience felt, and nothing else. It is silent on whether anyone can now interpret a confidence interval, which is the actual learning goal. The team has a usability-adjacent attitude metric standing in for a learning outcome it never measured. The number is real, but it is answering a question no one asked, and the unasked question (did they learn?) remains open.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nMetrics divide into two families because they measure two different constructs. Usability metrics measure operation; learning metrics measure capability change. Keeping them straight is the whole skill.\n\n| Family | Construct | Typical metrics | Cannot tell you |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Usability | Can people operate it? | task completion, time on task, error rate, satisfaction | Whether anyone learned |\n| Learning | Did capability change? | pre/post gain, transfer-item accuracy, retention | Whether the interface was pleasant |\n\nMatch each metric to the construct family it actually measures, so a claim can never quietly borrow validity from the wrong family.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Task completion rate\",\"definition\":\"Usability: whether people can operate the thing, silent on whether anyone learned\"},{\"term\":\"Time on task\",\"definition\":\"Usability: operability data that can rise or fall for reasons unrelated to learning\"},{\"term\":\"Pre/post gain on a target skill\",\"definition\":\"Learning: capability change, the metric a learning claim actually requires\"},{\"term\":\"Transfer-item accuracy\",\"definition\":\"Learning: applying the skill to a novel item, the Mayer standard for a meaningful outcome\"},{\"term\":\"Satisfaction rating\",\"definition\":\"Attitude: how the experience felt, which correlates weakly with learning and can drop on effortful lessons\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\nSatisfaction is the trap because it feels like a verdict on everything; in fact it is an attitude reading, and attitudes correlate weakly with learning. Learners often rate a hard, effortful lesson lower precisely because it made them think, which **cognitive load theory** explains as the difference between extraneous load (bad, lowers both satisfaction and learning) and germane load (the productive effort of building schemas, which can lower satisfaction while raising learning). A module that feels easy may have stripped out the very effort that produces durable knowledge.\n\nThis is why **Mayer multimedia learning** insists that the outcome metric target a meaningful, transferable performance rather than a vanity count. Time on a page is operability data; accuracy on a novel transfer item is learning data. **UDL learner variability** adds the final caution: a single mean can hide a split where the design helped advanced learners and stranded novices, so disaggregating outcomes by subgroup is part of measuring learning honestly, not an optional extra.\n\n## Worked Example: Separating a Usability Metric From a Learning Metric\n\nA designer reports results from testing a statistics-tutorial prototype.\n\n**Before (one metric standing in for everything):**\n\n> Result: 9 of 10 participants rated the module 4 or 5 out of 5 on satisfaction. The module works and learners are learning.\n\nWalk through why this single metric cannot carry both claims:\n\n1. **Classify each metric by what it measures.** Satisfaction is a usability/attitude metric. It measures how the experience felt, not whether anyone learned.\n2. **Name the learning claim separately.** \"Learners are learning\" is a learning-outcome claim and needs its own outcome measure (for example, pre/post performance on the target skill).\n3. **Pair each claim with the right metric.** Usability claim to usability metric (task completion, time on task, error rate, satisfaction); learning claim to a learning metric (pre/post gain, transfer item accuracy).\n4. **State each result against its own metric.**\n\n**After (matched claims and metrics):**\n\n> Usability: 8 of 10 completed the practice task unaided; mean time on task 2.4 minutes; 2 satisfaction ratings below 4 flagged the feedback panel. Learning: mean pre/post gain on the 5-item interpretation quiz was +1.8 items (3.1 to 4.9), with 7 of 10 improving.\n\nThe after version is defensible because each claim is backed by a metric that can actually measure it, and a high satisfaction score is no longer mistaken for a learning result. The before version committed a construct mismatch: it let an attitude rating overclaim a learning outcome.\n\nA learning claim lives or dies on the pre/post gain, and a single mean can hide a subgroup the design failed. Adjust the pre and post scores for a high-prior-knowledge group and a novice group below, and watch how the overall mean can stay healthy while one subgroup flatlines, the disaggregation check made visible.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Pre/post learning gain and the hidden-subgroup trap (5-item quiz)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"pre_high\",\"label\":\"High-prior group pre score\",\"min\":0,\"max\":5,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"items\",\"default\":3.4},{\"name\":\"post_high\",\"label\":\"High-prior group post score\",\"min\":0,\"max\":5,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"items\",\"default\":4.8},{\"name\":\"pre_nov\",\"label\":\"Novice group pre score\",\"min\":0,\"max\":5,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"items\",\"default\":2.8},{\"name\":\"post_nov\",\"label\":\"Novice group post score\",\"min\":0,\"max\":5,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"items\",\"default\":2.9}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"High-prior gain\",\"unit\":\"items\",\"expr\":\"post_high - pre_high\"},{\"label\":\"Novice gain\",\"unit\":\"items\",\"expr\":\"post_nov - pre_nov\"},{\"label\":\"Overall mean gain (reported headline)\",\"unit\":\"items\",\"expr\":\"(post_high + post_nov - pre_high - pre_nov) / 2\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"post_nov\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nSet the novice post score equal to its pre score: the novice gain goes to zero, yet the overall mean gain stays clearly positive on the strength of the high-prior group alone. That positive headline is exactly the construct-valid-looking number that conceals a design which lifted the prepared and stranded the unprepared.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\n<inline-check question=\"A demanding lesson earns LOWER satisfaction ratings than the easy version it replaced, but higher pre/post gain. What is the most defensible reading?\" explanation=\"Cognitive load theory distinguishes extraneous load (bad) from germane load, the productive effort of building schemas. A lesson that makes learners think can feel harder and rate lower while teaching more. The cure is to check the learning metric, not to soften the lesson; low satisfaction here is plausibly the signature of germane load.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The lesson is a usability failure and should be made easier\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Lower satisfaction may signal productive germane load; the gain shows it taught more\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Satisfaction and gain conflict, so both metrics are invalid\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Satisfaction should override the gain because learners disliked it\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nName the claim first, then choose the metric that has the validity to support that exact claim; never let a metric you happen to have collected define the claim you make. If your strongest evidence is satisfaction, your strongest defensible claim is about how the experience felt, not about whether it worked or taught.\n\nBefore locking metrics into your moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace, check three things:\n\n1. **Construct check:** Does each metric measure the construct the claim is about (operation versus learning)?\n2. **Proxy check:** Are you using satisfaction or completion as a stand-in for a learning outcome you never measured?\n3. **Disaggregation check:** Could a single mean be hiding a subgroup the design failed?\n\nIf any check fails, you are about to report a number that answers a different question than the one you are claiming.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe classic error is treating satisfaction as the master metric, so a well-liked module is declared effective without any outcome data. A second error is reading low satisfaction on a demanding lesson as a design failure, when it may be the signature of productive germane load; the cure for that is checking the learning metric, not softening the lesson. A third is reporting a flattering mean while a subgroup quietly failed, which a single number is structurally unable to reveal.\n\nA metric is an answer to one specific question; using it for another question is how plans overclaim.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTrace one claim from ambition to the metric that earns it. A team wants to assert that an interactive simulation teaches Ohm's law better than the static text it replaces. \"Better\" is a learning claim, so satisfaction and time on task are off the table; they can describe the experience but not the gain. The right instrument is a transfer measure: items that require applying the relationship to a circuit the learners never saw, scored pre and post, which is the **Mayer multimedia learning** standard for meaningful outcomes rather than recall of the worked examples themselves.\n\nNow read the result through **UDL learner variability**. Suppose the mean gain is healthy but driven entirely by learners with strong prior math; novices flatline. A disaggregated table turns that hidden split into a finding: the simulation helps the prepared and overloads the unprepared, which **cognitive load theory** would predict if the simulation's interface added extraneous demand that experts could absorb and novices could not. The walkthrough's lesson is that the learning claim and the usability claim must be reported side by side, because the usability metric (where novices stalled) often explains the learning metric (why novices did not gain).\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a results section, keep one column for each claim and one for the metric offered to support it, then mark every pairing where the metric measures a different construct than the claim. Those mismatches are where the report overclaims, and they are almost always a satisfaction or completion number doing a learning number's job.\n\nA strong reader of a metrics plan can answer four questions:\n\n1. For each claim, is the metric measuring operation or capability?\n2. Where is a proxy standing in for a learning outcome that was never measured?\n3. What does the satisfaction number actually license you to say?\n4. Could the headline mean be concealing a subgroup the design failed?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe measurement plan is strong when a reviewer can lay each claim beside its metric and confirm the metric could, on its own, disconfirm the claim. Treat the claim-to-metric pairing as the load-bearing element; a strong satisfaction result attached to a learning claim is not weak evidence but no evidence for that claim at all. Use the annotation tools to flag any claim whose only support is an attitude or completion number, and ask BigAL to compare your pairings against the construct check rather than to add more metrics.\n\nA useful closing line reads: \"I separated the usability and learning claims because a single satisfaction rating was carrying both; I rejected the suggestion to report only the overall mean gain, since disaggregating by prior knowledge revealed that novices did not improve.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same construct-matching discipline to a program-evaluation context, where the tempting proxy is not satisfaction but attendance or completion rate. A course with high completion has demonstrated that people finished it, not that they learned, exactly as high satisfaction demonstrated enjoyment, not learning. The structure transfers (name the claim, demand a metric with matching validity), but the seductive proxy changes with the setting. A reader who accepts completion as evidence of impact has made the same category mistake as the team that accepted satisfaction as evidence of learning.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/05-04-learning-metrics-and-usability-metrics-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"05.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **Mayer multimedia learning**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace for AIL 606 05.04. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to cognitive load theory. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm each claim is paired with a metric of matching construct, that no satisfaction or completion number is standing in for a learning outcome, and that means are disaggregated where a subgroup could be hidden.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics\", \"ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer multimedia learning. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics", "stem": "A team reports 90% satisfaction and concludes \"learners are learning.\" Which classification error did they make?", "options": ["Satisfaction is too small a percentage to report.", "They used a usability or attitude metric (satisfaction) to support a learning-outcome claim it cannot measure.", "Satisfaction should be reported as a mean, not a percentage.", "They needed more than 10 participants for satisfaction."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Satisfaction measures how the experience felt, not whether knowledge or skill changed; a learning claim needs a learning metric.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_satisfaction_as_learning", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics", "stem": "Which pairing correctly matches a claim to a metric that can measure it?", "options": ["Claim: the menu is findable. Metric: pre/post quiz gain.", "Claim: learners mastered the concept. Metric: average time on task.", "Claim: learners can apply the concept to new items. Metric: accuracy on transfer items not seen in the lesson.", "Claim: the interface is efficient. Metric: self-reported confidence."], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "A transfer claim is supported by performance on novel items; the other pairings attach a metric that measures a different construct.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_construct_mismatch", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "An AI dashboard auto-summarizes your session data into a single \"effectiveness score\" blending satisfaction, time, and quiz gain. What is the defensible boundary?", "options": ["Report the blended score because it is convenient and looks rigorous.", "Keep the metrics disaggregated; a single blended score hides whether a usability or a learning claim is actually supported.", "Trust the AI score over your own metrics.", "Delete the quiz data so the score is purely usability."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Blending distinct constructs into one number obscures which claim the evidence supports; keep usability and learning metrics separate.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_blended_score", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics", "stem": "Post-test scores rose, but participants also took the test immediately after viewing the answers in the lesson. Why is the learning gain claim still shaky?", "options": ["The sample size was too small.", "Without a delay or transfer measure, the gain may reflect short-term recall of just-seen answers rather than durable learning.", "Post-tests are never valid learning metrics.", "Satisfaction should have been measured instead."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "An immediate post-test can capture recall of fresh content; durable or transferable learning needs a delayed or novel-item measure.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_immediate_recall_as_learning", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_learning_metrics_and_usability_metrics"]}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_satisfaction_as_learning", "pattern": "satisfaction_as_learning_evidence", "description": "Using satisfaction or attitude ratings as evidence that learning occurred", "trigger": "learner reports high satisfaction and concludes the module taught the skill", "feedback": "Satisfaction is a usability and attitude metric. Support a learning claim with a learning metric such as pre/post or transfer performance.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_construct_mismatch", "pattern": "claim_metric_construct_mismatch", "description": "Pairing a claim with a metric that measures a different construct", "trigger": "learner backs a learning claim with a usability metric or vice versa", "feedback": "Match the metric to the construct: findability to task success or time, mastery to outcome scores, transfer to novel-item accuracy.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_blended_score", "pattern": "blended_single_score", "description": "Collapsing distinct usability and learning metrics into one composite number", "trigger": "learner reports a single effectiveness score combining different constructs", "feedback": "Keep the metrics disaggregated so a reader can see which specific claim each result supports.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_4_immediate_recall_as_learning", "pattern": "immediate_recall_as_durable_learning", "description": "Treating an immediate post-test gain as evidence of durable or transferable learning", "trigger": "learner claims durable learning from a test taken right after the content was shown", "feedback": "Add a delay or a transfer item so the gain is not just short-term recall of just-seen answers.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}